WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.498
Narration should sound like instruction, not like a policy document being read aloud.

2
00:00:05.498 --> 00:00:12.426
Good narration introduces context, explains relationships, highlights decisions, and guides attention.

3
00:00:12.426 --> 00:00:16.794
It can also create pacing by moving learners from one idea to the next.

4
00:00:16.794 --> 00:00:22.744
The script should be clear enough to listen to, but specific enough to teach something useful.

5
00:00:22.744 --> 00:00:26.660
Avoid simply duplicating dense on-screen text word for word.

6
00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:33.814
When learners hear the same text they are trying to read, the experience can feel redundant and mentally crowded.

7
00:00:33.814 --> 00:00:42.701
A better pattern is to keep on-screen text concise and let narration explain the meaning, example, consequence, or practical application.

8
00:00:42.701 --> 00:00:47.596
Captions should remain available so learners can access the spoken content.

9
00:00:47.596 --> 00:00:49.479
Media should be intentional.

10
00:00:49.479 --> 00:00:58.139
Voiceover, captions, images, icons, video, diagrams, and screen recordings can all improve learning when they support the objective.

11
00:00:58.139 --> 00:01:00.851
They can also distract when used as filler.

12
00:01:00.851 --> 00:01:04.390
A diagram may clarify a process better than a paragraph.

13
00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:06.800
A short video may help with a workflow.

14
00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:11.394
An icon may help scanning, but it should not replace needed explanation.

15
00:01:11.394 --> 00:01:17.645
Learner engagement comes from relevance, examples, pacing, and application more than gimmicks.

16
00:01:17.645 --> 00:01:23.594
A course feels engaging when learners see why the topic matters and can connect it to real work.

17
00:01:23.594 --> 00:01:26.079
Keep media organized and replaceable.

18
00:01:26.079 --> 00:01:36.698
File naming, source tracking, and version control at a practical level make future updates easier when policies, products, screenshots, or voiceover need to change.

19
00:01:36.698 --> 00:01:41.593
For narration workflows, keep the script connected to the module structure.

20
00:01:41.593 --> 00:01:49.426
Label scripts by module and screen, track approved versions, and avoid recording before the content is reasonably stable.

21
00:01:49.426 --> 00:02:00.044
Re-recording is sometimes necessary, but disciplined script review prevents unnecessary audio churn and helps captions stay aligned with the final spoken message.
